The code statements that lead to the bug are:
while ((qla2x00_reset_active(vha) || ha->dpc_active ||
	       ha->flags.mbox_busy) ||
	       test_bit(FX00_RESET_RECOVERY, &vha->dpc_flags) ||
	       test_bit(FX00_TARGET_SCAN, &vha->dpc_flags)) {
		if (test_bit(UNLOADING, &base_vha->dpc_flags))
			break;
		msleep(1000);
	}
Reason:
The while loop condition did not check `vha->scan.scan_flags & SF_SCANNING`, so when the UNLOADING flag was set and a fabric scan was in progress, the loop could exit early (via the break) without waiting for the scan to complete. During the ongoing scan, an interrupt might trigger a callback (e.g., `qla24xx_sp_unmap`) that attempted to free DMA memory (`dma_free_attrs`) in interrupt context, because the work item for scan completion was not allocated due to the UNLOADING flag. This caused a system crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
	while ((qla2x00_reset_active(vha) || ha->dpc_active ||
		ha->flags.mbox_busy) ||
	       test_bit(FX00_RESET_RECOVERY, &vha->dpc_flags) ||
	       test_bit(FX00_TARGET_SCAN, &vha->dpc_flags) ||
	       (vha->scan.scan_flags & SF_SCANNING)) {
		if (test_bit(UNLOADING, &base_vha->dpc_flags))
			break;
		msleep(1000);
	}
Reason:
Adding the `SF_SCANNING` check ensures that the driver unload is delayed until any ongoing fabric scan has completed (i.e., the scanning flag is cleared). This prevents the race condition where the unload proceeds before the scan finishes, thereby avoiding the invalid DMA free in interrupt context. The loop now properly waits for all scan-related activities to end before allowing the UNLOADING break to take effect.

